Sounds like you've made decent progress so far.  That minetown lawful
altar could come in handy for a post-quest self conversion.  I suspect
self conversion makes protection go *poof*, but can't recall from
personal experience.

> Here's the problem, if I descend any farther I'll have an average of
> XP and dungeon level of 7 whereupon I'm sure to start seeing soldier
> ants.  And I don't think that my current AC is sufficient protection
> from insects.

Under ordinary circumstances, I'd say you're golden in the mid-game if
your dungeon depth is less than XL - AC + (weapon enchantment).  An
illiterate without a few ?scare monster should be more cautious.  An
instrument capable of scaring monsters is another big win.

> The most obvious options (within the arbitary limits of illiterate and
> racial) seem to be:
> 1) kill the Minetown priest and do some Kop farming, although I've
> never done this so don't know what to expect (except for a lot of
> cream pies);

I wouldn't, you may need this altar for self conversion later.  Unless
you plan on HoOA-ing yourself to lawful, then reconverting the altar
(bye-bye protection!).  That might violate your "racial armour"
restricition, however.

> 2) descend into the mines farther and try to find a poly trap, the
> risk here is that I'm going to get surrounded without a magic whistle
> and potentially run out of food;
> 3) surround myself with my pets and then fountain dip for a wish
>
> I suppose the reason I haven't gone immediately for option 3 is that I
> don't really know what to wish for:
> ~~  a magic whistle,
> ~~ blessed fixed +3 orcish foo
> ~~ Platinum Yendorian Express Card (grants MR and charging ... haven't
> seen a neutral altar yet in the game though ... and this doesn't
> contribute to short term survival)

I would recommend wishing for a tooled horn or leather drum.  The
scaring effect comes in quite handy.  If you use a wish for MR and
haven't found a  neutral altar yet, I'd recommend either the Sceptre
of Might (conflict) or the Orb of Detection (half spell damage +
toggles invisibility).  The Magic Mirror of Merlin is mostly useless
to a non-knight.

